<section class="ea-lab" data-sampling aria-label="Repeated sampling experiment">
  <header class="ea-lab-heading"><div><h3>Precision can make an error convincing.</h3><p>100 nominal 95% intervals; target mean = 0.</p></div><span class="ea-live">Seeded simulation</span></header>
  <figure class="ea-figure"><svg data-sampling-plot viewBox="0 0 530 340" role="img" aria-label="100 confidence intervals with a vertical reference line at the true target zero"><text x="30" y="50" font-size="14">The simulation will appear when JavaScript is enabled.</text></svg><figcaption>Solid teal intervals cover zero. Dashed rust intervals miss. The horizontal scale stays fixed as sample size changes; a boundary arrow marks a clipped endpoint.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="ea-controls"><label class="ea-control">Observations in each study <output data-n-value for="sampling-n">50</output><input id="sampling-n" data-sampling-n type="range" min="20" max="1000" step="10" value="50" /></label><label class="ea-control">Selection bias, in outcome standard deviations <output data-bias-value for="sampling-bias">0.00</output><input id="sampling-bias" data-sampling-bias type="range" min="0" max=".8" step=".05" value="0" /></label></div>
  <div class="ea-lab-readouts" aria-live="polite"><div>Intervals covering the target<strong data-coverage>…</strong></div><div>Interval half-width<strong data-width>…</strong></div></div>
  <div class="ea-lab-foot"><p data-sampling-verdict></p><div class="ea-lab-buttons"><button class="ea-button" data-new-sample>Run another 100 studies</button><button class="ea-button secondary" data-sampling-download>Download results CSV</button></div><p class="ea-small" data-seed></p></div>
  <details><summary>Exact simulation, deliberately narrow assumptions</summary><p>Each study samples n independent normal observations with known variance 1 and observed mean bias. We draw the sample mean directly from Normal(bias, 1/n), exactly its sampling distribution. The interval is mean ± 1.959964/√n. The policy target remains zero; positive bias represents a mismatch between the sampled population and that target.</p><p>Coverage is random across batches. Under zero bias the procedure has 95% coverage; 95 of 100 is not guaranteed. Unknown variance, dependent data, nonnormality, and estimated selection adjustments require different inference. This is a known-sigma illustration, not an OLS standard-error calculator.</p></details>
</section>
